{"type": "message", "id": "e0a08a21", "parentId": "2b3a2b82", "timestamp": "2026-04-07T19:34:30.720Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I have confirmed data from SEC filings. Let me compile the quarterly financial data:\n\nFrom the Tesla Q1 2024 Quarterly Update (SEC filing):\n- Q1 2023: Total revenues = $23,329 million, Net income = $2,539 million\n- Q2 2023: Total revenues = $24,927 million, Net income = $2,614 million\n- Q3 2023: Total revenues = $23,350 million, Net income = $1,878 million\n- Q4 2023: Total revenues = $25,167 million, Net income = $7,943 million\n\nNow let me calculate the net profit margin percentages:\n\n\nWorking through each quarter, the net profit margins are Q1 at 10.88%, Q2 at 10.49%, Q3 at 8.04%, and Q4 at 31.56%. Q4's abnormally high margin reflects a one-time non-cash tax benefit of $5.9 billion from releasing valuation allowances on deferred tax assets.\n\nFor non-GAAP net income in Q4, the figure drops to $2.5 billion, which would put the margin around 9.94%. But since the question asks for quarterly data from the SEC filings, I should use the GAAP numbers reported directly: Q1 revenue of $23,329 million with net income of $2,539 million gives 10.88%, Q2 revenue of $24,927 million with net income of $2,614 million gives 10.49%, and Q3 starts at $23,350 million in revenue with $1,878 million in net income.\n\nThe Q4 figures show $25,167 million in revenue and $7,943 million in GAAP net income, which calculates to 31.56% - quite high due to that one-time tax benefit. Since I'm asked for GAAP figures from the SEC filings, Q4 clearly exceeds 10.5%, so the answer includes Q1, Q3, and Q4. I'm verifying the calculation is correct—net income divided by revenue times 100 gives 10.88% for Q1, which exceeds the 10.5% threshold. Now I need to confirm whether to use GAAP or non-GAAP net income figures from the SEC filings, since the question asks for data as reported. I'm looking at the 10-K filing which will have all the quarterly breakdowns I need. Since the question asks about SEC quarterly statements including both 10-Qs and 10-Ks, I should use the GAAP net income numbers as they're officially reported. The math checks out: Q1 shows $2,539M net income on $23,329M revenue for 10.88%, which exceeds the 10.5% threshold, while Q2 comes in just below at 10.49%.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_67e2a05362784701bfa16b26", "name": "web_search", "arguments": {"count": 10, "query": "Tesla 10-K 2023 annual report quarterly income statement net income revenue Q1 Q2 Q3 Q4"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "openrouter", "model": "xiaomi/mimo-v2-omni", "usage": {"input": 31521, "output": 1406, "cacheRead": 22456, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 55383, "cost": {"input": 0.012608399999999999, "output": 0.002812, "cacheRead": 0.00179648, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.01721688}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1775590462414, "responseId": "gen-1775590462-7APJV1wkNNf87GsVxZjU"}}
